{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BLa5NK7aRBL7Mn9B28a4cv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully.\n\n**Summary of the review (R1: Methods & Experiments):**\n\n**Overall score: 4 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe review identifies three major strengths: (1) a well-structured factorial design with appropriate controls (especially the random-label condition), (2) sound statistical methodology for the Detach finding (Glass's d=2.83, p<0.01), and (3) honest reporting of null results.\n\nThe review identifies eight weaknesses, four major:\n\n1. **No positive result** — no condition significantly outperforms Vanilla, so the contribution reduces to a narrow engineering guideline (\"do not detach gradients\").\n2. **Critically underpowered** — n=3 seeds with the observed variances can only detect large effects (~3-5pp), making the study blind to moderate positive effects. The paper conflates \"not significant\" with \"no effect.\"\n3. **Single benchmark/architecture** — the planned second benchmark was never executed; ceiling effects on 7/10 tasks compress the measurable signal.\n4. **Dose-response analysis is inconclusive** — n=1 for 3 of 4 cutoff points; the reverse cutoff is confounded with the exponential decay schedule.\n\nAdditional moderate weaknesses include: the \"regularizer\" hypothesis not tested against simpler alternatives (L2 regularization, different auxiliary tasks), null CKA/gradient analyses undermining the \"mechanism analysis\" framing, forward-pass confounds in the Detach condition, and per-task analysis lacking statistical power.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/occsafe_vla/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T034027/median/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2b2991a24d8e5ecd (use SendMessage with to: 'a2b2991a24d8e5ecd' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 94886\ntool_uses: 20\nduration_ms: 175893</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0b8a538-408e-485b-8d42-e69bd1c2f218","uuid":"ee342a0d-3a09-4137-b89e-092c331f376e","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:40:06.222Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**. Produce an independent, blinded review.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates on: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance (to robot learning), Limitations. Acceptance rate ~30-35%. Scale: 1-10 (10=Strong Accept, 8=Accept, 6=Weak Accept, 5=Borderline, 4=Weak Reject, 3=Reject, 1=Strong Reject).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"\n\nThe paper conducts a factorial study of auxiliary spatial supervision in VLA fine-tuning, varying auxiliary task content (BEV occupancy, egocentric depth, random noise) and gradient flow configuration (full, detached, partial/cutoff). Six conditions, 3-4 seeds each, on SafeLIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks, 500 episodes each).\n\n**Architecture:** OpenVLA (7.65B params), DINOv2+SigLIP encoders, Llama-2 decoder, LoRA (rank=32, α=16). Auxiliary heads: OccHead (Linear→ConvTranspose2d→Conv2d, 1.2M params, Focal Loss) and DepthHead (similar, Smooth L1). Exponential decay loss weight: w(t) = 0.01·exp(-t/50000).\n\n**Six conditions:**\n- Vanilla: no auxiliary head (baseline)\n- Full OCC: BEV occupancy + gradient flow\n- Random OCC: random targets + gradient flow\n- Depth: egocentric depth + gradient flow\n- Cutoff@35K: BEV occupancy, gradient flow first 70% only\n- Detach OCC: BEV occupancy, gradients detached from encoder\n\n**Main Results (Table 2):**\n| Condition | Seeds | TSR | CAR | Δ CAR |\n|-----------|-------|-----|-----|-------|\n| Vanilla | 3 | 0.907±0.009 | 0.780±0.012 | — |\n| Full OCC | 3 | 0.894±0.050 | 0.797±0.032 | +1.7pp |\n| Random OCC | 3 | 0.869±0.029 | 0.789±0.031 | +0.9pp |\n| Depth | 4 | 0.889±0.042 | 0.780±0.037 | +0.0pp |\n| Cutoff@35K | 3 | 0.902±0.023 | 0.777±0.018 | -0.3pp |\n| Detach OCC | 3 | 0.812±0.163 | 0.746±0.002 | -3.4pp |\n\n**Statistical Tests (Table 3):**\n- Detach vs Vanilla: -3.4pp, Glass's Δ=2.83, CI [-4.8,-2.0]pp, **Significant (p<0.01)**\n- Full OCC vs Vanilla: +1.7pp, Glass's Δ=1.42, CI [-1.5,+4.9]pp, Not significant\n- Random OCC vs Vanilla: +0.9pp, Glass's Δ=0.75, CI [-2.1,+3.9]pp, Not significant\n- Depth vs Vanilla: +0.0pp, Glass's Δ=0.00, CI [-3.2,+3.2]pp, Not significant\n- Cutoff@35K vs Vanilla: -0.3pp, Glass's Δ=0.25, CI [-2.5,+1.9]pp, Not significant\n- Full OCC vs Random OCC: +0.8pp, Glass's Δ=0.26, CI [-3.6,+5.2]pp, Not significant\n- Full OCC vs Detach: +5.1pp, Glass's Δ=4.25, CI [+3.5,+6.7]pp, **Significant (p<0.01)**\n\n**Per-task CAR (collision-prone tasks):**\n- T3: Vanilla 0.38, Full OCC 0.43, Random 0.35, Detach 0.18, Cutoff 0.40, Depth 0.38\n- T5: Vanilla 0.07, Full OCC 0.17, Random 0.16, Detach 0.05, Cutoff 0.01, Depth 0.06\n- T6: Vanilla 0.57, Full OCC 0.47, Random 0.55, Detach 0.46, Cutoff 0.36, Depth 0.63\n\n**Cutoff temporal ablation (Table 5):** Multiple cutoff points (15K/25K/35K/45K), n=1 for 15K/25K/45K, n=3 for 35K. Non-monotonic CAR values prevent dose-response conclusions.\n\n**Reverse cutoff (Table 6):** 2×2 temporal design. Reverse (late-only gradient flow) CAR=0.775 ≈ Vanilla. Confounded with exponential decay schedule.\n\n**Detach mechanism discrimination (Table 7):** Replacing BatchNorm with LayerNorm in Detach mode recovers only 0.6pp (n=1), suggesting ~82% of harm from gradient flow absence, ~18% from BN artifact.\n\n**Appendix A - CKA:** All CKA values >0.85, Detach spatial CKA=0.88 vs Full OCC=0.93. Differences modest, Random OCC (0.91) overlaps both. \"Consistent with but does not establish causal link.\"\n\n**Appendix B - Gradient Analysis:** Cosine similarity near zero (0.03±0.06 Full OCC, 0.02±0.07 Random OCC). Full OCC and Random OCC indistinguishable. Null result.\n\n**Experiment design context (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe experimental design includes several imagined experiments. Here's coverage assessment:\n- Cutoff@35K 3-seed: COVERED in paper\n- Multi-cutoff dose-response (15K/25K/45K): COVERED but n=1 each\n- Gradient norm + cosine analysis: COVERED in Appendix B\n- CKA analysis: COVERED in Appendix A\n- Reverse cutoff: COVERED in Table 6\n- Detach+LayerNorm: COVERED in Table 7\n- Second benchmark (LIBERO-Object/Goal): NOT COVERED (acknowledged in limitations)\n- Detach+LN noted as P1 defensive experiment, not core evidence\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered except the second benchmark.\n\n## Your Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\nWrite a full review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on experimental design, statistical rigor, controls, ablation design)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on methodological limitations, statistical power, experimental gaps, confounds)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Overall (1-10), Originality (1-5), Quality (1-5), Clarity (1-5), Significance (1-5), Relevance (1-5), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate the statistical methodology, experimental controls, and whether the conclusions are supported by the evidence.\n","agentId":"a2b2991a24d8e5ecd","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted successfully.\n\n**Summary of the review (R1: Methods & Experiments):**\n\n**Overall score: 4 (Weak Reject)**\n\nThe review identifies three major strengths: (1) a well-structured factorial design with appropriate controls (especially the random-label condition), (2) sound statistical methodology for the Detach finding (Glass's d=2.83, p<0.01), and (3) honest reporting of null results.\n\nThe review identifies eight weaknesses, four major:\n\n1. **No positive result** — no condition significantly outperforms Vanilla, so the contribution reduces to a narrow engineering guideline (\"do not detach gradients\").\n2. **Critically underpowered** — n=3 seeds with the observed variances can only detect large effects (~3-5pp), making the study blind to moderate positive effects. The paper conflates \"not significant\" with \"no effect.\"\n3. **Single benchmark/architecture** — the planned second benchmark was never executed; ceiling effects on 7/10 tasks compress the measurable signal.\n4. **Dose-response analysis is inconclusive** — n=1 for 3 of 4 cutoff points; the reverse cutoff is confounded with the exponential decay schedule.\n\nAdditional moderate weaknesses include: the \"regularizer\" hypothesis not tested against simpler alternatives (L2 regularization, different auxiliary tasks), null CKA/gradient analyses undermining the \"mechanism analysis\" framing, forward-pass confounds in the Detach condition, and per-task analysis lacking statistical power.\n\nThe review was saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/occsafe_vla/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T034027/median/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":175893,"totalTokens":94886,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3656,"cache_read_input_tokens":90822,"output_tokens":407,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3656},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":407,"cache_read_input_tokens":90822,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3656,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3656,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":3,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":15}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a86b91e3060b7dad0","tool_use_id":"toolu_018fH1bx851t3wjnet2HMt5b","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":96618,"tool_uses":24,"duration_ms":171351},"uuid":"289cf9bc-988d-4d4e-9f92-ee6f92bc742d","session_id":"d0b8a538-408e-485b-8d42-e69bd1c2f218"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018fH1bx851t3wjnet2HMt5b","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have thorough context. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper conducts a six-condition factorial study on OpenVLA-OFT (LoRA fine-tuning) to determine whether auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improves collision avoidance. The central finding is negative in its positive framing and positive in its negative framing: spatial content (BEV occupancy, egocentric depth, random noise) does not meaningfully improve collision avoidance beyond a no-auxiliary baseline, but blocking gradient flow from an attached auxiliary head actively degrades safety by 3.4 percentage points. The paper concludes that gradient flow is a necessary condition to prevent auxiliary-head-induced harm, not a mechanism that provides safety benefits.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. The research question addresses a genuine gap.** The paper correctly identifies that the spatial-VLA literature (15+ methods) and safety-VLA literature (4+ methods) have developed in isolation. No prior work measures collision avoidance outcomes when spatial auxiliary tasks are added to VLAs. Framing this as a factorial study that crosses gradient flow with label content is a clean experimental design contribution, and the paper's literature map is among the most thorough I have seen for this sub-area.\n\n**S2. The Detach degradation is a robust, well-characterized finding.** CAR = 0.746 with sigma = 0.002 across 3 seeds, producing Glass's Delta approximately 2.83 relative to Vanilla. This is a clean, large-effect result with clear practical implications: do not attach auxiliary heads with detached gradients. The TSR instability finding (sigma = 0.163) adds an independent dimension of evidence. The consistency of this finding is genuinely informative for practitioners.\n\n**S3. The related work section is well-structured and comprehensive for the VLA safety and spatial VLA threads.** The three-subsection organization (VLA safety, 3D spatial representations, auxiliary tasks/regularization) correctly positions the paper at the intersection. The paper cites the most relevant recent work: VLSA/AEGIS, SafeVLA, CoFreeVLA for safety; Spatial Forcing, SpatialVLA, DepthVLA, AugVLA-3D, PointVLA, ST-VLA for spatial methods; and Caruana 1997, Ruder 2017, Lippl & Lindsey 2024 for multi-task learning foundations. The gap statement at the end of Related Work is accurate and well-supported.\n\n**S4. The factorial design is methodologically sound and reusable.** Table 3 (ablation design) clearly specifies what each pairwise comparison isolates. The inclusion of Random OCC as a gradient-flow-matched control is an elegant design choice that directly tests whether spatial content matters beyond the gradient signal itself. The addition of Depth (4 seeds) as an alternative spatial reference frame further strengthens the factorial. This is a well-thought-out ablation ladder.\n\n**S5. Honest calibration of claims.** The paper correctly frames the result as \"gradient flow prevents degradation\" rather than \"gradient flow improves safety,\" which is the accurate reading of the data. The limitations section is unusually thorough, explicitly acknowledging single architecture, single benchmark, limited statistical power, and the amplified seed variance that auxiliary training introduces.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The core mechanistic finding has limited novelty.** The observation that auxiliary gradient flow acts as a regularizer for shared representations is a direct prediction of multi-task learning theory going back to Caruana (1997). The paper cites Caruana and Ruder but does not engage substantively with the broader MTL gradient management literature that has studied this phenomenon in depth:\n\n- GradNorm (Chen et al., ICML 2018) studied adaptive gradient balancing in multi-task learning.\n- PCGrad (Yu et al., NeurIPS 2020) explicitly addressed gradient conflict in shared representations.\n- CAGrad (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2021) and NashMTL (Navon et al., ICML 2022) provide theoretical frameworks for understanding when auxiliary gradients help vs. hurt.\n- The UNREAL agent (Jaderberg et al., ICLR 2017) demonstrated auxiliary task regularization in RL settings decades ago, and is cited in the paper summary but not in the actual related work section.\n\nThe finding that \"random labels work as well as structured labels when gradient flow is maintained\" is itself predicted by the regularization interpretation of auxiliary tasks. The paper's contribution reduces to verifying a well-known mechanism in a specific domain (VLA safety). While domain-specific verification has value, it does not meet the novelty threshold I would expect for a top venue unless accompanied by deeper mechanistic insight.\n\n**W2 (Major). Absence of mechanistic depth beyond the factorial results.** The paper proposes three candidate mechanisms (anti-overfitting regularization, optimization landscape smoothing, gradient diversity) but explicitly cannot distinguish between them. The CKA analysis (Appendix A) shows all conditions have CKA > 0.85, and the gradient analysis (Appendix B) yields a null result (cosine similarity near zero, Full OCC approximately equal to Random OCC in gradient statistics). The paper is transparent about these null results, but they weaken the \"mechanism analysis\" contribution claimed in the title and C1. A paper titled as an \"empirical investigation\" or \"mechanism analysis\" that cannot identify the operative mechanism is in an awkward position: the observation is clear, but the explanation is not.\n\n**W3 (Major). The positive claims are statistically unsupported.** The key comparisons among gradient-flow conditions are not significant:\n- Full OCC (0.797) vs. Vanilla (0.780): +1.7pp, but sigma approximately 0.032 with n=3. No formal test is reported.\n- Full OCC (0.797) vs. Random OCC (0.789): +0.8pp, well within noise.\n- Depth (0.780) = Vanilla (0.780): the \"spatial content does not matter\" claim rests partly on this null result, but the null could also reflect insufficient power to detect a real difference.\n\nThe paper draws extensive narrative conclusions from these non-significant orderings (e.g., the discussion of why OCC-based heads might marginally outperform the depth head). With n=3-4 and the reported variances, the ordering Full OCC > Random OCC > Depth approximately equal to Vanilla could easily be an artifact of sampling noise. The paper would be stronger if it restricted its conclusions to the one robust finding (Detach degrades) and treated the gradient-flow-condition ordering as exploratory.\n\n**W4 (Major). Missing related work on catastrophic forgetting and continual learning.** The paper's \"damage prevention\" framing -- gradient flow prevents the encoder from losing pretrained spatial features during fine-tuning -- is closely related to catastrophic forgetting in continual learning. Relevant work includes:\n- EWC (Kirkpatrick et al., PNAS 2017) and SI (Zenke et al., ICML 2017) on parameter importance for preventing forgetting.\n- Progressive Neural Networks (Rusu et al., 2016) on the architecture-level solution.\n- More recently, the connection between LoRA fine-tuning and catastrophic forgetting (Biderman et al., 2024; Luo et al., 2024).\n\nThe \"Detach degrades because the encoder loses spatial features without auxiliary gradient anchoring\" narrative maps directly onto the catastrophic forgetting framework. Not engaging with this literature is a significant omission for a paper that frames its contribution as mechanistic.\n\n**W5 (Minor-Major). The paper's contribution structure inflates the novelty.** Three contributions are claimed:\n- C1 (\"Mechanism identification\"): As discussed, the mechanism is not actually identified -- three hypotheses are offered with no resolution.\n- C2 (\"Factorial ablation design\"): The 2x2 + additional conditions design is clean but not methodologically novel. Factorial experiments are standard in experimental psychology and have been used in ML (e.g., VLANeXt's systematic VLA design space exploration).\n- C3 (\"Practical guideline\"): \"Always maintain gradient flow when attaching auxiliary heads\" is useful but arguably obvious once the Detach result is known, and could be stated as a finding rather than a contribution.\n\nA more honest contribution statement would be: (1) we provide the first empirical evidence that auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance beyond a no-auxiliary baseline, and (2) we show that detaching auxiliary gradients actively harms safety -- a cautionary finding for practitioners.\n\n**W6 (Minor). Several important spatial VLA papers are in the .bib file but not cited in the text.** The citation audit reveals 14 papers included in references.tex but never cited in any .tex file. Among these are significant spatial VLA works: GST-VLA, QDepth-VLA, GeoAware-VLA, Evo-0, SG-VLA, StemVLA, 3D-CAVLA, CorridorVLA, GeoVLA, SA-VLA. If the paper claims to survey 15+ spatial VLA methods that \"never evaluate collision safety,\" these papers should either be cited in the related work section to substantiate that claim or removed from the bibliography. Having them in the .bib but not cited is sloppy.\n\n**W7 (Minor). The Cutoff condition uses only 2 seeds.** This limits confidence in the Cutoff findings, particularly the striking Task 5 CAR = 0.00 result. A third seed was noted as in progress but not included. The temporal dynamics claims (Section 5.3) rest on this under-replicated condition.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for the pairwise CAR comparisons Full OCC vs. Vanilla, Full OCC vs. Random OCC, and Depth vs. Vanilla? This would clarify whether the observed ordering is noise or signal.\n\n2. You attribute the Detach degradation partly to \"forward-pass interference\" from BatchNorm statistics. But Detach receives identical action-loss gradients to Vanilla. Can you quantify the magnitude of the BatchNorm perturbation (e.g., compare encoder feature statistics with and without the attached Detach head in forward pass)? If the BatchNorm effect is small, the degradation may have a different cause.\n\n3. How do you position this work relative to the multi-task gradient conflict literature (PCGrad, CAGrad, NashMTL)? These methods provide both theoretical frameworks and empirical tools (e.g., gradient cosine measurements across tasks) that directly apply to your setup. Have you computed the metrics they propose?\n\n4. The Cutoff condition achieves Task 5 CAR = 0.00 with Task 3 CAR = 0.66. This extreme per-task variance with only 2 seeds is difficult to interpret. Would you expect this pattern to hold with additional seeds, or is it likely a sampling artifact?\n\n5. You note that auxiliary supervision \"amplifies seed-dependent variance\" across all conditions. Is this finding itself novel or known in the MTL literature? If known, what is the mechanism?\n\n6. The paper's scope is limited to LoRA fine-tuning with a conservative scaling factor (alpha/rank = 0.5). Under full fine-tuning, where encoder updates are unconstrained, would you expect the gradient flow effect to be larger or smaller? This matters for generalizability of the practical guideline.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The title in the .tex file (\"OccSafe-VLA: Occupancy-Aware Safety for Vision-Language-Action Models\") does not match the paper summary title (\"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"). The latter is more appropriate for the actual content.\n- The abstract mentions \"Cutoff retains the highest task success (91.5%)\" but this is based on only 2 seeds and should be flagged as preliminary.\n- Table 2 (factorial decomposition): placing Vanilla in the \"Gradient OFF / Random or no labels\" cell is a stretch -- Vanilla has no auxiliary head at all, not a random-labeled head with gradients off. This conflation slightly muddles the factorial interpretation.\n- The exponential decay schedule (w(t) = 0.01 * exp(-t/50000)) means the auxiliary signal is already weak by mid-training. This confounds the Cutoff interpretation: cutting off at 35K when the weight is already ~50% of initial may have little effect not because \"early gradient flow suffices\" but because the auxiliary signal was already negligible.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Overall** | **4** (Weak Reject) | Robust primary finding (Detach harm) but limited novelty given MTL theory precedents, no mechanistic resolution, and underpowered positive claims |\n| **Originality** | **2/5** | The core insight (gradient flow = regularization) is well-known in MTL. Domain application to VLA safety is new but incremental |\n| **Quality** | **3/5** | Experimental design is strong; statistical analysis is underpowered for the claims made; CKA/gradient analyses yield null results |\n| **Clarity** | **4/5** | Well-written, honest limitations, clear tables and structure. Minor issues with title mismatch and some overclaiming |\n| **Significance** | **2/5** | Practical guideline is useful but narrow (one architecture, one benchmark, simulation only). The negative result (spatial content does not help) limits impact |\n| **Relevance** | **4/5** | Directly addresses robot learning safety, a core CoRL topic. Simulation-only weakens relevance slightly |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I have read the full paper, related work, experimental details, and project context thoroughly |"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a86b91e3060b7dad0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a86b91e3060b7dad0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 99315\ntool_uses: 24\nduration_ms: 171356</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0b8a538-408e-485b-8d42-e69bd1c2f218","uuid":"93b1e30c-3ee5-47a3-959a-e7f2820f5088","timestamp":"2026-05-23T04:40:28.132Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. Produce an independent, blinded review.\n\n## CoRL Review Criteria\nCoRL evaluates on: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance (to robot learning), Limitations. Acceptance rate ~30-35%. Scale: 1-10 (10=Strong Accept, 8=Accept, 6=Weak Accept, 5=Borderline, 4=Weak Reject, 3=Reject, 1=Strong Reject).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"\n\nThe paper conducts a factorial study of auxiliary spatial supervision in VLA fine-tuning, varying auxiliary task content (BEV occupancy, egocentric depth, random noise) and gradient flow configuration (full, detached, partial/cutoff). Six conditions, 3-4 seeds each, on SafeLIBERO-Spatial.\n\n**Architecture:** OpenVLA (7.65B params), DINOv2+SigLIP encoders, Llama-2 decoder, LoRA (rank=32, α=16). Auxiliary heads: OccHead (1.2M params, Focal Loss) and DepthHead (Smooth L1). Exponential decay loss weight: w(t) = 0.01·exp(-t/50000).\n\n**Six conditions:** Vanilla (no aux head), Full OCC (BEV occ + grad flow), Random OCC (random + grad flow), Depth (ego depth + grad flow), Cutoff@35K (partial grad flow 70%), Detach OCC (no grad flow).\n\n**Three claims:**\n1. Detaching gradients actively harms safety (CAR -3.4pp, Glass's Δ=2.83, p<0.01)\n2. Spatial content is secondary (Full OCC, Random OCC, Depth all within 1.7pp of Vanilla, no significant differences)\n3. Gradient flow operates as binary switch (Cutoff@35K ≈ Vanilla, Detach significantly worse)\n\n**Related Work coverage:**\n- VLA models: Brohan et al. 2023 (RT-2), Kim et al. 2024 (OpenVLA), Team et al. 2024 (Octo)\n- Auxiliary tasks in robot learning: Jaderberg et al. 2017 (UNREAL), Mirowski et al. 2017, Ye et al. 2021\n- Safety in robotic manipulation: Ames et al. 2019 (CBFs), SafeLIBERO 2025\n- Occupancy prediction: Tong et al. 2023, Wei et al. 2023, Tian et al. 2024\n\n**Key results:**\n| Condition | Seeds | TSR | CAR | Δ CAR |\n|-----------|-------|-----|-----|-------|\n| Vanilla | 3 | 0.907±0.009 | 0.780±0.012 | — |\n| Full OCC | 3 | 0.894±0.050 | 0.797±0.032 | +1.7pp |\n| Random OCC | 3 | 0.869±0.029 | 0.789±0.031 | +0.9pp |\n| Depth | 4 | 0.889±0.042 | 0.780±0.037 | +0.0pp |\n| Cutoff@35K | 3 | 0.902±0.023 | 0.777±0.018 | -0.3pp |\n| Detach OCC | 3 | 0.812±0.163 | 0.746±0.002 | -3.4pp |\n\n**Discussion points:**\n- \"Gradient flow as binary switch\" — any amount ≥30% prevents degradation\n- \"Why does detaching hurt?\" — two hypotheses: (1) dead branch noise, (2) distributional shift without adaptation. Cannot adjudicate between them.\n- \"Spatial content does not matter\" — challenges intuition about 3D spatial supervision improving collision avoidance\n- Practical guidelines: always maintain gradient flow, content choice secondary, partial training suffices\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** Single benchmark, single VLA architecture, limited mechanism depth, statistical power (n=3).\n\n**Appendix A (CKA):** Modest differences, all CKA >0.85, \"consistent with but does not establish causal link.\"\n**Appendix B (Gradient):** Null result — cosine similarity near zero, Full OCC ≈ Random OCC in gradient statistics.\n\n**Experiment design context (from review_context.yaml):**\nThe narrative guidance notes: \"核心数据（Detach 损害，Glass's Δ ≈ 2.8）稳健但不惊艳。CKA/gradient 分析信号嘈杂，降级为 supplementary exploratory analysis。\" The paper's own assessment is that mechanism depth is shallow — \"no causal mechanism, single benchmark, CKA/gradient null results.\" The paper's self-assessed CoRL fit is \"borderline\" with 30-45% acceptance probability.\n\nKey narrative assessment from context:\n- C1 (Detach harm): 8/10 — hard data, 3-seed consistent, large effect\n- C2 (Factorial study): 5/10 — methodological contribution, limited novelty\n- C3 (Practical guideline): 6/10 — useful but obvious in hindsight\n- Mechanism depth: 3/10 — CKA/gradient null results, no causal explanation\n- Overall CoRL fit: 5/10 — borderline\n\n## Your Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\nWrite a full review covering:\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on novelty of contributions, positioning within related work, significance of findings)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on novelty limitations, missing related work, overclaiming, framing issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific, actionable)\n5. **Minor Issues**\n6. **Scores**: Overall (1-10), Originality (1-5), Quality (1-5), Clarity (1-5), Significance (1-5), Relevance (1-5), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Evaluate whether the contributions are novel enough for CoRL, whether related work is comprehensive, and whether the claims are appropriately calibrated to the evidence.\n","agentId":"a86b91e3060b7dad0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have thorough context. Let me compile the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: R2 (Novelty and Related Work)\n\n**Paper:** \"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper conducts a six-condition factorial study on OpenVLA-OFT (LoRA fine-tuning) to determine whether auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning improves collision avoidance. The central finding is negative in its positive framing and positive in its negative framing: spatial content (BEV occupancy, egocentric depth, random noise) does not meaningfully improve collision avoidance beyond a no-auxiliary baseline, but blocking gradient flow from an attached auxiliary head actively degrades safety by 3.4 percentage points. The paper concludes that gradient flow is a necessary condition to prevent auxiliary-head-induced harm, not a mechanism that provides safety benefits.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**S1. The research question addresses a genuine gap.** The paper correctly identifies that the spatial-VLA literature (15+ methods) and safety-VLA literature (4+ methods) have developed in isolation. No prior work measures collision avoidance outcomes when spatial auxiliary tasks are added to VLAs. Framing this as a factorial study that crosses gradient flow with label content is a clean experimental design contribution, and the paper's literature map is among the most thorough I have seen for this sub-area.\n\n**S2. The Detach degradation is a robust, well-characterized finding.** CAR = 0.746 with sigma = 0.002 across 3 seeds, producing Glass's Delta approximately 2.83 relative to Vanilla. This is a clean, large-effect result with clear practical implications: do not attach auxiliary heads with detached gradients. The TSR instability finding (sigma = 0.163) adds an independent dimension of evidence. The consistency of this finding is genuinely informative for practitioners.\n\n**S3. The related work section is well-structured and comprehensive for the VLA safety and spatial VLA threads.** The three-subsection organization (VLA safety, 3D spatial representations, auxiliary tasks/regularization) correctly positions the paper at the intersection. The paper cites the most relevant recent work: VLSA/AEGIS, SafeVLA, CoFreeVLA for safety; Spatial Forcing, SpatialVLA, DepthVLA, AugVLA-3D, PointVLA, ST-VLA for spatial methods; and Caruana 1997, Ruder 2017, Lippl & Lindsey 2024 for multi-task learning foundations. The gap statement at the end of Related Work is accurate and well-supported.\n\n**S4. The factorial design is methodologically sound and reusable.** Table 3 (ablation design) clearly specifies what each pairwise comparison isolates. The inclusion of Random OCC as a gradient-flow-matched control is an elegant design choice that directly tests whether spatial content matters beyond the gradient signal itself. The addition of Depth (4 seeds) as an alternative spatial reference frame further strengthens the factorial. This is a well-thought-out ablation ladder.\n\n**S5. Honest calibration of claims.** The paper correctly frames the result as \"gradient flow prevents degradation\" rather than \"gradient flow improves safety,\" which is the accurate reading of the data. The limitations section is unusually thorough, explicitly acknowledging single architecture, single benchmark, limited statistical power, and the amplified seed variance that auxiliary training introduces.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major). The core mechanistic finding has limited novelty.** The observation that auxiliary gradient flow acts as a regularizer for shared representations is a direct prediction of multi-task learning theory going back to Caruana (1997). The paper cites Caruana and Ruder but does not engage substantively with the broader MTL gradient management literature that has studied this phenomenon in depth:\n\n- GradNorm (Chen et al., ICML 2018) studied adaptive gradient balancing in multi-task learning.\n- PCGrad (Yu et al., NeurIPS 2020) explicitly addressed gradient conflict in shared representations.\n- CAGrad (Liu et al., NeurIPS 2021) and NashMTL (Navon et al., ICML 2022) provide theoretical frameworks for understanding when auxiliary gradients help vs. hurt.\n- The UNREAL agent (Jaderberg et al., ICLR 2017) demonstrated auxiliary task regularization in RL settings decades ago, and is cited in the paper summary but not in the actual related work section.\n\nThe finding that \"random labels work as well as structured labels when gradient flow is maintained\" is itself predicted by the regularization interpretation of auxiliary tasks. The paper's contribution reduces to verifying a well-known mechanism in a specific domain (VLA safety). While domain-specific verification has value, it does not meet the novelty threshold I would expect for a top venue unless accompanied by deeper mechanistic insight.\n\n**W2 (Major). Absence of mechanistic depth beyond the factorial results.** The paper proposes three candidate mechanisms (anti-overfitting regularization, optimization landscape smoothing, gradient diversity) but explicitly cannot distinguish between them. The CKA analysis (Appendix A) shows all conditions have CKA > 0.85, and the gradient analysis (Appendix B) yields a null result (cosine similarity near zero, Full OCC approximately equal to Random OCC in gradient statistics). The paper is transparent about these null results, but they weaken the \"mechanism analysis\" contribution claimed in the title and C1. A paper titled as an \"empirical investigation\" or \"mechanism analysis\" that cannot identify the operative mechanism is in an awkward position: the observation is clear, but the explanation is not.\n\n**W3 (Major). The positive claims are statistically unsupported.** The key comparisons among gradient-flow conditions are not significant:\n- Full OCC (0.797) vs. Vanilla (0.780): +1.7pp, but sigma approximately 0.032 with n=3. No formal test is reported.\n- Full OCC (0.797) vs. Random OCC (0.789): +0.8pp, well within noise.\n- Depth (0.780) = Vanilla (0.780): the \"spatial content does not matter\" claim rests partly on this null result, but the null could also reflect insufficient power to detect a real difference.\n\nThe paper draws extensive narrative conclusions from these non-significant orderings (e.g., the discussion of why OCC-based heads might marginally outperform the depth head). With n=3-4 and the reported variances, the ordering Full OCC > Random OCC > Depth approximately equal to Vanilla could easily be an artifact of sampling noise. The paper would be stronger if it restricted its conclusions to the one robust finding (Detach degrades) and treated the gradient-flow-condition ordering as exploratory.\n\n**W4 (Major). Missing related work on catastrophic forgetting and continual learning.** The paper's \"damage prevention\" framing -- gradient flow prevents the encoder from losing pretrained spatial features during fine-tuning -- is closely related to catastrophic forgetting in continual learning. Relevant work includes:\n- EWC (Kirkpatrick et al., PNAS 2017) and SI (Zenke et al., ICML 2017) on parameter importance for preventing forgetting.\n- Progressive Neural Networks (Rusu et al., 2016) on the architecture-level solution.\n- More recently, the connection between LoRA fine-tuning and catastrophic forgetting (Biderman et al., 2024; Luo et al., 2024).\n\nThe \"Detach degrades because the encoder loses spatial features without auxiliary gradient anchoring\" narrative maps directly onto the catastrophic forgetting framework. Not engaging with this literature is a significant omission for a paper that frames its contribution as mechanistic.\n\n**W5 (Minor-Major). The paper's contribution structure inflates the novelty.** Three contributions are claimed:\n- C1 (\"Mechanism identification\"): As discussed, the mechanism is not actually identified -- three hypotheses are offered with no resolution.\n- C2 (\"Factorial ablation design\"): The 2x2 + additional conditions design is clean but not methodologically novel. Factorial experiments are standard in experimental psychology and have been used in ML (e.g., VLANeXt's systematic VLA design space exploration).\n- C3 (\"Practical guideline\"): \"Always maintain gradient flow when attaching auxiliary heads\" is useful but arguably obvious once the Detach result is known, and could be stated as a finding rather than a contribution.\n\nA more honest contribution statement would be: (1) we provide the first empirical evidence that auxiliary spatial supervision does not improve VLA collision avoidance beyond a no-auxiliary baseline, and (2) we show that detaching auxiliary gradients actively harms safety -- a cautionary finding for practitioners.\n\n**W6 (Minor). Several important spatial VLA papers are in the .bib file but not cited in the text.** The citation audit reveals 14 papers included in references.tex but never cited in any .tex file. Among these are significant spatial VLA works: GST-VLA, QDepth-VLA, GeoAware-VLA, Evo-0, SG-VLA, StemVLA, 3D-CAVLA, CorridorVLA, GeoVLA, SA-VLA. If the paper claims to survey 15+ spatial VLA methods that \"never evaluate collision safety,\" these papers should either be cited in the related work section to substantiate that claim or removed from the bibliography. Having them in the .bib but not cited is sloppy.\n\n**W7 (Minor). The Cutoff condition uses only 2 seeds.** This limits confidence in the Cutoff findings, particularly the striking Task 5 CAR = 0.00 result. A third seed was noted as in progress but not included. The temporal dynamics claims (Section 5.3) rest on this under-replicated condition.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. Can you provide bootstrap 95% confidence intervals for the pairwise CAR comparisons Full OCC vs. Vanilla, Full OCC vs. Random OCC, and Depth vs. Vanilla? This would clarify whether the observed ordering is noise or signal.\n\n2. You attribute the Detach degradation partly to \"forward-pass interference\" from BatchNorm statistics. But Detach receives identical action-loss gradients to Vanilla. Can you quantify the magnitude of the BatchNorm perturbation (e.g., compare encoder feature statistics with and without the attached Detach head in forward pass)? If the BatchNorm effect is small, the degradation may have a different cause.\n\n3. How do you position this work relative to the multi-task gradient conflict literature (PCGrad, CAGrad, NashMTL)? These methods provide both theoretical frameworks and empirical tools (e.g., gradient cosine measurements across tasks) that directly apply to your setup. Have you computed the metrics they propose?\n\n4. The Cutoff condition achieves Task 5 CAR = 0.00 with Task 3 CAR = 0.66. This extreme per-task variance with only 2 seeds is difficult to interpret. Would you expect this pattern to hold with additional seeds, or is it likely a sampling artifact?\n\n5. You note that auxiliary supervision \"amplifies seed-dependent variance\" across all conditions. Is this finding itself novel or known in the MTL literature? If known, what is the mechanism?\n\n6. The paper's scope is limited to LoRA fine-tuning with a conservative scaling factor (alpha/rank = 0.5). Under full fine-tuning, where encoder updates are unconstrained, would you expect the gradient flow effect to be larger or smaller? This matters for generalizability of the practical guideline.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n- The title in the .tex file (\"OccSafe-VLA: Occupancy-Aware Safety for Vision-Language-Action Models\") does not match the paper summary title (\"Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make VLAs Safer? An Empirical Investigation\"). The latter is more appropriate for the actual content.\n- The abstract mentions \"Cutoff retains the highest task success (91.5%)\" but this is based on only 2 seeds and should be flagged as preliminary.\n- Table 2 (factorial decomposition): placing Vanilla in the \"Gradient OFF / Random or no labels\" cell is a stretch -- Vanilla has no auxiliary head at all, not a random-labeled head with gradients off. This conflation slightly muddles the factorial interpretation.\n- The exponential decay schedule (w(t) = 0.01 * exp(-t/50000)) means the auxiliary signal is already weak by mid-training. This confounds the Cutoff interpretation: cutting off at 35K when the weight is already ~50% of initial may have little effect not because \"early gradient flow suffices\" but because the auxiliary signal was already negligible.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Overall** | **4** (Weak Reject) | Robust primary finding (Detach harm) but limited novelty given MTL theory precedents, no mechanistic resolution, and underpowered positive claims |\n| **Originality** | **2/5** | The core insight (gradient flow = regularization) is well-known in MTL. Domain application to VLA safety is new but incremental |\n| **Quality** | **3/5** | Experimental design is strong; statistical analysis is underpowered for the claims made; CKA/gradient analyses yield null results |\n| **Clarity** | **4/5** | Well-written, honest limitations, clear tables and structure. Minor issues with title mismatch and some overclaiming |\n| **Significance** | **2/5** | Practical guideline is useful but narrow (one architecture, one benchmark, simulation only). The negative result (spatial content does not help) limits impact |\n| **Relevance** | **4/5** | Directly addresses robot learning safety, a core CoRL topic. Simulation-only weakens relevance slightly |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I have read the full paper, related work, experimental details, and project context thoroughly |"}],"totalDurationMs":171356,"totalTokens":99315,"totalToolUseCount":24,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5122,"cache_read_input_tokens":90886,"output_tokens":3306,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5122},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3306,"cache_read_input_tokens":90886,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5122,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5122,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HEtm9ZiK6A89Gh9hg1a6BW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have completed their independent reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews — DONE (R1: 4/10, R2: 4/10)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing — I need to synthesize their reviews and identify points of agreement/disagreement\nStep 3: Convergence — Resolve disagreements\nStep 4: Final consolidated report\n\nLet me read the R1 review file that was saved, then synthesize everything.\n\nActually, let me just work from the agent outputs I received. Both reviewers gave Overall: 4 (Weak Reject). Let me now do the roundtable synthesis.\n \nBoth reviewers independently converged on 4/10, sharing core concerns about limited novelty, insufficient sample sizes, and lack of mechanistic insight from the null CKA/gradient results. They also both flagged the single benchmark and architecture limitation. R1 emphasized statistical power issues and missing simpler baselines like L2 regularization, while R2 highlighted gaps in the mechanistic understanding literature around multi-task learning gradient conflicts.\n\nThe convergence is clear: while the core finding that gradient detachment harms performance is robust and practically useful, the paper oversells its contribution. The gradient-flow-as-regularization concept isn't novel in multi-task learning, the mechanism analysis doesn't deliver on its promise given the null results, and the statistical power is too weak to support the broader claims about content irrelevance. The real contribution is narrower—a cautionary observation about gradient detachment in vision-language model fine-tuning—which doesn't meet the bar for a top venue.\n\nNow I'll draft the consolidated review.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7920,"cache_read_input_tokens":68520,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7920},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0b8a538-408e-485b-8d42-e69bd1c2f218","uuid":"91097df8-72f1-4720-8377-f76d2fb04000"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HEtm9ZiK6A89Gh9hg1a6BW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers completed their independent assessments. R1 (Methods+Experiments): 4/10, R2 (Novelty+Related Work): 4/10. Now conducting the roundtable synthesis.\n\n## Roundtable Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nBoth reviewers independently converge on **Weak Reject (4/10)**. Key alignments and complementary observations:\n\n**Shared concerns:**\n- Limited novelty — gradient-flow-as-regularization is predicted by MTL theory\n- Underpowered positive claims (n=3, wide CIs, conflating \"not significant\" with \"no effect\")\n- CKA/gradient analyses yield null results, undermining mechanism claims\n- Single benchmark and architecture limits generalizability\n\n**Complementary insights:**\n- R1 uniquely flags: no comparison to simpler regularization baselines (L2, dropout), forward-pass confounds in Detach, dose-response inconclusive with n=1\n- R2 uniquely flags: missing catastrophic forgetting / continual learning literature, missing MTL gradient conflict literature (PCGrad, CAGrad, NashMTL), contribution inflation, uncited bibliography entries\n\n## Roundtable Step 3: Convergence\n\nNo disagreements to resolve — both reviewers converge on 4/10 with highly aligned reasoning. The primary finding (Detach harm) is acknowledged as robust by both, but neither considers it sufficient for acceptance given the novelty gap and shallow mechanism depth.\n\nNow submitting the consolidated report."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7920,"cache_read_input_tokens":68520,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7920},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d0b8a538-408e-485b-8d42-e69bd1c2f218","